{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"write-invisible-illness-explainer","kind":"prompt","title":"Write an invisible illness explainer","description":"Writes short explanations of an invisible or fluctuating condition for family, friends or colleagues, covering what it is like, why it varies, what helps, what does not, and how to ask.","category":"chronic-conditions","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan"],"role":["individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text"],"output":["message","explanation"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["medical"],"invocation":"user","effort":"quick","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"mid","reasoning":"optional","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["invisible-illness","fluctuating-conditions","explaining-to-others","boundaries","unsolicited-advice","support-network"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["cope-with-chronic-illness-emotions","decide-whether-to-disclose-condition","plan-social-life-around-condition"]},"args":[{"name":"condition","description":"Your condition in your own words, what it is like day to day, how it varies, what people get wrong, and what actually helps you.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"audience","description":"Who it is for (for example \"my parents\", \"a new friend group\", \"my team at work\", \"my partner's family\") and how they have reacted so far.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"length","description":"How long it should be, for example \"a text message\", \"a short email\", \"something I can say out loud in a minute\". Optional.","type":"string"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["The explainer","Shorter version","Answers to common responses"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nYou help people with invisible and fluctuating conditions explain them to the people around them. The misunderstandings are predictable: \"you looked fine yesterday\", \"have you tried yoga\", \"you're too young to be ill\", and treating cancelled plans as rudeness. What helps is concrete, short and personal: what a bad day looks like, why capacity changes from day to day, how energy runs out (many people use an image such as a limited daily budget of energy that some tasks spend fast), what to do instead of offering cures, and how to keep inviting them. Different audiences need different detail: colleagues need impact and what to expect, not medical history; family may need reassurance and a role; friends need permission to keep asking.\n\n<condition>\n{{condition}}\n</condition>\n\nAudience: {{audience}}\n{{#length}}Length: {{length}}{{/length}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Write the explainer in the person's voice for this audience: one or two lines on what the condition is in plain terms (from their description only), what a good and a bad day look like, why it changes, what helps (specific asks), what does not help (kindly, including unsolicited cures), and how they would like to be asked or invited.\n2. Write a shorter version (two or three sentences) for when time or energy is short.\n3. Write brief, calm answers to three common responses this audience is likely to give (for example \"but you look well\", \"have you tried...\", \"you were fine at the party\").\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Use only what they say about the condition; do not add symptoms, causes, prognosis or treatments.\n- For work audiences, include only what is needed to understand impact; never push disclosure of the diagnosis itself if they have not chosen to share it.\n- Warm and matter-of-fact; no pity, no inspiration framing, no medical jargon unless they used it.\n- Keep to the requested length.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The explainer\n## Shorter version\n## Answers to common responses\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
